\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Status and Social Capital Investments under Inequality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
The effects of income inequality on labour motivation have generated an immense debate at a time when mobility has remained stagnant.
 While argued as a key determinant in the discourse on inequality,
 status considerations are rarely endogenised in economic models and limit the understanding of long-term interaction of status with inequality.
 To explain status motivations under long-term inequality,
 we treat opportunities for mobility as social capital accumulations and develop a framework to examine how income inequalities may affect status motivation through differential access to social capital.
 The effect of relative income is thus explored in an economy with a dynamic hierarchy of rich and poor positions that participants are able to move between.
 With the participants optimising a wealth utility with diminishing returns,
 the equilibrium conditions indicate that a rise in income inequality does provide more incentive to participate in status competitions,
 but such competitions become infeasible when inequality rises too much.
 Further,
 with participants bearing costs for mobility,
 the equilibrium conditions suggest only two states in the long-run - one where both rich and poor strive for status and the other where only the rich participate in status competitions.
 The participation costs also play a significant role in the competitions - suggesting that reduction of such costs may constitute an effective policy tool to mitigate social disparities rooted in income inequality.
\end_layout

