\begin_layout Standard
The effect of relative income on consumer behaviour has long engaged economists - but the mechanisms through which relative income or perceived inequalities shape labor participation remain contested.
 Some perspectives frame inequality as a necessary incentive structure that rewards effort and stimulates mobility 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{BaronIncIneqMotivation2017}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 while others emphasize its discouraging effects on participation and long-term productivity 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{GesiarzIneqMotiv2020}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The debate is acutely reflected in education policy as well:
 inequality may function as an incentive that rewards effort through mobility,
 but it also acts as a barrier when education primarily serves as a signaling device 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{AryalBullerLange2021,Durst2023,JaegerPage1995,HungerfordSolon1987}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Status motivations lie at the heart of these debates.
 Individuals’ investments in education and other forms of social capital respond both to perceived social position (subjective status) and to structural opportunities (objective status).
 While empirical measurements of status have been highly effective in cross-sectional analyses of inequality,
 the measurement challenges with status tend to obscure long-term effects of inequality on status-driven decisions 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{wegener1992prestigeconcepts}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Education policy,
 in particular,
 faces a recurring dilemma:
 expanding access by lowering participation costs versus preserving excellence by rewarding high performers.
 So long as education can be assumed to carry some signaling value,
 status considerations cannot be ignored when assessing how inequality shapes mobility as well as education expenditures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This paper addresses these issues by endogenizing status in an economic model for social capital investments – incorporating the signaling value of education that may be reflected in networks and professional affiliations.
 The model considers a dynamic hierarchy of positions with relative economic advantage where participants can move through probabilistic tournaments combining raw ability and acquired social capital.
 The presented framework allows us to examine how income differences interact with participation costs in shaping mobility-related investments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Three main findings emerge.
 First,
 while inequality may initially encourage social capital investment,
 excessive inequality imposes limits that make status competitions unsustainable.
 Second,
 participation costs are central:
 in the context of social capital assisted mobility through small-scale competitions (interviews,
 business-introductions etc.),
 high participation costs can offset the benefits of reduced inequality and higher income differences may sustain competitions with low participation costs.
 Third,
 the model produces only two stable equilibria:
 one where both rich and poor invest in status-related capital,
 and another where only the rich do.
 These results echo the broader literature on the tension between equality of opportunity and equality of outcome 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{HeckmanMosso2014}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 while clarifying the role of status as an endogenous link between inequality and education policy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The small-scale tournaments with varied success criteria across several stages that we consider in the paper are relevant for many interactions where social capital investments play a significant role (e.g.
 in interviews,
 formal introductions and business interactions).
 The equilibrium conditions explore a single instance of a competition with known distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 among participants who exercise their choice to spend on social capital without an 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
emph{a priori}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 knowledge of 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 In what follows,
