\begin_layout Standard
The relationship between inequality,
 motivation,
 and status-seeking behavior has generated extensive debate across both economics and sociology.
 While early economic thought often emphasized the functional role of inequality as a motivational force—
providing incentives for effort,
 investment,
 and innovation—
more recent perspectives have challenged this view,
 particularly in the context of constrained social mobility and stratified opportunity structures.
 Arguments in favor of inequality 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{BaronIncIneqMotivation2017}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 often emphasize efficiency and incentive compatibility 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{BeckerTomesEquilibMobility1979}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 based on the idea that greater rewards reflect greater effort.
 However,
 competing empirical and behavioral research suggests that beyond a certain threshold,
 inequality can undermine labor participation,
 erode social trust,
 and reduce long-term productivity 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{PerottiGrowthIncomeDis1993,AlesinaPerotti1996,StiglitzPriceofIneq2012}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 These contrasting perspectives remain visible in recent debates,
 reflecting an unresolved tension:
 whether inequality spurs economic participation or discourages it by limiting realistic pathways to advancement 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{ChettyLandofOpportunity2014}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
==EDUCATION==
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang british
Inequality has long been tied to education,
 often motivating policies aimed at expanding access to schooling.
 The conventional argument is that greater access fosters mobility and reduces disparities.
 However,
 the argument against expanding education for social welfare appears is that signaling value of education may amount wasteful expenditure rather than a means to enhance productivity 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{AryalBullerLange2021,Durst2023,HungerfordSolon1987}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The tension underpins a continuing debate on whether inequality stimulates effort by rewarding mobility,
 or suppresses it by raising barriers and entrenching stagnation.
 Our finding highlights an interplay of participation costs and income gaps - where high costs can negate the benefits of reduced inequality,
 while large income differences may sustain participation despite unequal opportunities 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{HeckmanMosso2014}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
==STATUS==
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite status or prestige being a key motivator for labour participation
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{BenabouTiroleIntrinsicExtrinsic2003,FrankPond}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 the conventional view of status is one where it is contrasted as a social goal against the economic goals of a consumer – a dichotomy that complicates the endogenisation of status within economic models.
 
\lang british
Empirical work shows that both talent and access to resources shape future success,
 complicating attempts to isolate purely economic effects.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{CamboniNiuPaiVohra2021}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 highlight how institutional monitoring and effort interact with heterogeneous student types.
 The research underscores the dual economic and social character of social capital,
 which mediates how inequality interacts with long-term educational investments 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{Jencks1979}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 A rich literature also addresses the signaling function of education.
 Spence’s seminal framework 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{Spence1973}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 established how credentials convey information,
 while subsequent studies show that signaling effects differ across institutional contexts 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{Durst2023,Heisig}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
noindent
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang british
These perspectives contrast with human capital theory,
 which views education primarily as skill acquisition.
 Complementary insights from Weberian traditions,
 Jencks 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{Jencks1979}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 and Gamoran 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{Gamoran2000}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 highlight that educational success depends on both innate characteristics and social participation,
 while labor-queue theory from 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{Thurow1975}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 frames workers as competing for positional advantages rather than wages.
 The present paper builds on this literature by integrating insights from signaling and social capital into a model where status is conceptualized as a dynamic position maintained through both ability and social capital investments - subject to income inequality.
 This formulation enables a more nuanced understanding of how inequality and participation costs interact,
 and how localized reference-dependent utilities may allow status competitions to persist even under unequal conditions.
 This allows a clearer view of how inequality and participation costs jointly influence long-term mobility,
 situating the analysis within the broader debate on whether inequality motivates or constrains effort.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang british
==
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The framework also allows us to reconcile the objective and subjective aspects of status in the argument for participation.
 We argue that neither is status orthogonal to economic goals nor it is derived purely from economic rewards 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{moldovanuselacontestsstatus2007}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and explicitly define the scope of the effects of income inequality on consumer motivations to participate.
 The competition-based view of status – drawing from 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{goode1978celebrationheroes}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 – emphasises how institutions discourage or promote the success-determining attributes in a society.
 While status influences economic outcomes,
 it is not viewed in the model as a wealth-determined goal.
 Instead,
 using an approach proposed by 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeA{postlewaite1998socialbasisinterdeppreferences}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 status is considered only of an indirect role in maximisation of wealth utility – so that the rank of the participant (rich or poor status) does not directly contribute to utility and matters only through a higher income that contributes to maximisation of the participant's expected wealth utility.
\end_layout

