\begin_layout Subsubsection
Intertemporal Choice
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The participants optimise an intertemporally additive utility over their life-time - which is represented with two time-horizons.
 The second-time horizon is set to be one before the end of life when all wealth accumulated by either participant must disappear.
 With no accumulations at the end of life,
 both rich and poor participants must decide to spend all their incomes 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 (for poor and rich participants respectively so that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}<y_{2}$
\end_inset

) towards the promotion game.
 In the first-stage,
 however,
 the participants decide the amount to be spent towards promotion based upon the utility from wealth they obtain across the two time-horizons that span their lifetime.
 For the poor participant,
 the total utility to be optimised (assuming EUT) under the intertemporally additive utility 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

,
 a winning probability function 
\begin_inset Formula $W(x)$
\end_inset

 (for expenditure 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

) and a discount-factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 is 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
u(y_{1}-\nu_{1})+\frac{1}{\delta}((1-W(y_{1}))u(y_{1})+W(y_{1})u(y_{2}))
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly,
 for the rich participant,
 the total utility to be optimised (assuming EUT) is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
u(y_{2}-\nu_{2})+\frac{1}{\delta}((1-W(y_{2}))u(y_{1})+W(y_{2})u(y_{2}))
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

