\begin_layout Standard
This paper relies on a Weberian framework - where status is endogenised as a position of power that directly provides economic gains - position that are relevant in choice of education,
 networks,
 neighborhood selection and professional affiliations.
 Using a decision theory framework for uncertainty inherent in status considerations,
 the paper then investigates the role of income gaps on social capital expenditures towards mobility - which education is a key part of.
 The endogenisation of status in this manner allows an understanding of the role of long-term inequality on social capital expenditures under a signaling view of education.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With participation costs held constant,
 a rise in inequality increases social capital expenditures due to competition.
 While the signaling view of education suggests that expansion of education-access amplifies socially wasteful expenditures,
 such argument cannot ignore how status considerations themselves change with respective to inequality in the long-run.
 Much of status consumption literature suggests that status considerations are sensitive to changes in inequality.
 If rise in inequality causes status considerations to become more important (corresponding to a rise in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

),
 then the social capital expenditures for the poor (rich) would rise (decline).
 Likewise,
 if status considerations become less important (less 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

),
 then social capital expenditures would decline (rise) for the poor (rich).
 In equilibrium,
 the participation costs must rise (decline) with decline (rise) in inequality to either allow only rich to participate for both rich and poor to participate.
 Status considerations are thus hardly external to such arguments.
 Societies rarely adopt the extreme position where living-standards are made uniform at the cost of meritocracy.
 Therefore status consideration,
 participation costs and inequality remain intricately linked so long as status competitions exist in a society.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper highlights three important details pertaining to social capital investments such as education in an environment of income inequalities.
 The first detail is the role of a reference-based utility – which serves as a caveat to the effects of income-differences on social capital expenditures.
 The model shows that contentment at lower levels of wealth could support segregation of competitions at different income levels in the economy.
 In other words,
 competitions for objective status could be feasible despite vast income differences so long as the utility depends on local levels of wealth.
 This also seems in line with many of the findings from subjective contentment data on belief in meritocracy amdist inequalities 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{MijsMeritocracy2020}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second detail highlighted by the model is the interplay of participation costs (threshold) and income-differences in competitions for objective status.
 The model explains how a withdrawal from expenditures such as education could be the result of a combined effect of both participation costs and income-differences.
 More specifically,
 high participation costs push the equilibrium to one where only rich participate in status-related competitions while high income differences may continue to incentivise the poor to participate.
 In other words,
 reducing income-differences without lowering of participation costs in status competitions may have an effect of supporting status-related competitions under long-established inequalities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Lastly,
 there are only two long-term states of the poor-rich relationship that exist for competitions for status under a diminishing wealth utility - one where both rich and poor participate in competitions for objective status and another where only rich stand to benefit from such competitions.
 The particular detail demonstrates how income differences limit the benefit from competitions to the poor - providing a quantitative basis for observations on social limits to growth in the literature 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{HirschSocialLimits,GalbraithBeyondContentment1994,GalbraithAffluent}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 More specifically,
 while poor may be incentivised with status in a setting where status is exclusive to the rich,
 the other long-term equilibrium is one where both rich and poor must strive for status.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The view of status as dynamic outcome of social capital investments reveals clear limits on how inequality affects status-seeking through mobility-related expenditures such as education.
 The role of participation costs and reference-based utility in sustaining competitions despite high levels of inequality suggest in particular that signaling value of education must be considered when targeting participation costs and inclusive mobility as targets for policy.
\end_layout

