\backslash
section{Convolution of Uniform Random Variables} 
\backslash
label{sec:appendix_theorertical_1}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The distribution of a convex combination 
\begin_inset Formula $w=\mu r+(1-\mu)s$
\end_inset

 of two random variables 
\begin_inset Formula $r,s$
\end_inset

 - which are distributed according to densities 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{R}(r)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{S}(s)$
\end_inset

 - can be derived using the convolution of two derived variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x=\mu r$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y=(1-\mu)s$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 are uniformly distributed variables ranging from 0 to 1,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 ranges from 0 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 ranges from 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\mu)$
\end_inset

.
 The CDF for 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{X}(x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{Y}(y)$
\end_inset

 are simply 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{X}(x)=\frac{x}{\mu}$
\end_inset

 ,
 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{Y}(y)=\frac{y}{1-\mu}$
\end_inset

 (respectively).
 Therefore,
 the densities for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{X}(x)=\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{Y}(y)=\frac{1}{1-\mu}$
\end_inset

.
 The density of 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 is simple 
\begin_inset Formula $f(w)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f_{Y}(w-x)f_{X}(x)dx$
\end_inset

.
 Since ,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{X}(x)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\mu$
\end_inset

 only between 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

.
 We have the following integral to evaluate for the convolution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
f(w) & =\frac{1}{\mu}\int_{0}^{\mu}f_{Y}(w-x)dx
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{Y}(y)$
\end_inset

 is 1 only between 0 and 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\mu$
\end_inset

,
 we have the condition 
\begin_inset Formula $0<w-x<1-\mu\Rightarrow w-(1-\mu)<x<w$
\end_inset

.
 Further,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is distributed only between 0 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

.
 As shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:convex_combo"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 the integral would evaluate to the following
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Notice that as a convex combination,
 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 is distributed only between 0 and 1.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 under the assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu<\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
f(w)=\frac{1}{\mu}\int_{0}^{\mu}f_{Y}(w-x)dx & =\begin{cases}
\frac{1}{\mu}\int_{0}^{w}\frac{1}{1-\mu}dx & 0<w<\mu\\
\frac{1}{\mu}\int_{0}^{\mu}\frac{1}{1-\mu}dx & \mu<w<1-\mu\\
\frac{1}{\mu}\int_{w-(1-\mu)}^{\mu}\frac{1}{1-\mu}dx & 1-\mu<w<1\\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This can be simplified as 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
f(w) & =\begin{cases}
\frac{w}{\mu(1-\mu)} & 0<w<\mu\\
\frac{1}{1-\mu} & \mu<w<1-\mu\\
\frac{1-w}{\mu(1-\mu)} & 1-\mu<w<1\\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:convex_combo_plot"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 shows this trapezoidal density with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 set to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{5}$
\end_inset

.
 The CDF 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{X}(x)$
\end_inset

 can be evaluated by integrating the above density as 
\begin_inset Formula $P(X<x)=\int_{-\infty}^{x}f(t)dt$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
F_{X}(x) & =\begin{cases}
\frac{x^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)} & 0<x<\mu\\
\frac{\mu}{2(1-\mu)}+\frac{x-\mu}{1-\mu} & \mu<x<1-\mu\\
\frac{\mu^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)}+\frac{2\mu(1-2\mu)}{2\mu(1-\mu)}+\frac{\mu^{2}-x^{2}+2x-1}{2\mu(1-\mu)} & 1-\mu<x<1\\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Simplifying above,
 we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
F_{X}(x) & =\begin{cases}
\frac{x^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)} & 0<x<\mu\\
\frac{x-\mu/2}{1-\mu} & \mu<x<1-\mu\\
1-\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2\mu(1-\mu)} & 1-\mu<x<1\\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

